# The interaction design of <the application> — <the increment of its model>

## Before you write: what the delivery kit reads in this document

*This section is the template's, not the document's. Delete it from the document you write; the
kit does not read it.*

This is the kit's template of the interaction design, the document the standard **SDD-11, The
Interaction Design**, governs. The document settles, once for the goals one writing of the
application model carries, how a person picks a coded value, finds one record among many, moves a
record from one state to the next, and acts on a record; the owner accepts it; and the application
model names it, in its entry `model.interaction_design`, by its path relative to the model and its
SHA-256 checksum. SDD-11 fixes the seven parts of the document and the headings of its five
decision tables (its section 7). **This template fixes the rest of what the kit's gate needs: how a
row names the construct of the application model it governs, and which words the gate reads in each
column it compares** (SDD-11, section 8.2, last paragraph). The gate is rule L021 of the kit's
validator, `tools/validate.py`, and it refuses the model — in `kit validate`, `kit gen`, the build
and the deploy, in every custody mode — wherever the model disagrees with what is read here.

**Why a row names the model's identifier.** The document is accepted before the model is written,
and the model is then written from it. So every row names the construct it governs by the
identifier the model gives, or will give, that construct, written between single backticks: a list
by the identifier of its vocabulary, a record by its entity, a move by its transition, a role, a
form, a field. Most of these identifiers stand upstream already — the lists, records and their
attributes in the entity model, the states and moves in the shared groundwork — and the document
names them as they stand there. Where one does not stand yet, such as the identifier of a form, the
document gives it, and the writer of the model uses it. **The words for the owner stay in the same
cell, before the identifier**: "Debt category (`debt_category`)". The owner reads the words; the
gate reads the identifier.

**What the gate reads, and nothing else.** It reads identifiers only between single backticks, and
only in the cells named below. In the cells it compares it reads only the words named below, as
the first such word of the cell, whatever case they are written in. Every other cell, and every
other word of these cells, is for the owner and the writer of the model, and the gate does not read
it. A cell may hold a vertical bar written `\|`.

**What makes the gate refuse the reading itself.** A table the gate reads that is missing, or that
stands twice, or whose headings are not written exactly as below; a row with more or fewer cells
than its table has columns; a row that names no construct in the cell where it must, or names more
than one; a cell the gate compares that holds none of the words it reads. Each is refused with the
line of the document it stands on. A row may instead say, in its naming cell, **not in the model**:
the gate then reads nothing else of it. Use it only for a thing the goals show and the model does
not carry as a construct of that table's kind — a set of values shown and never chosen, which the
model keeps as text — and say why in the row.

### The header — part 1

| Where | What the gate reads |
|---|---|
| The table headed `Line` · `Entry` | the row whose Line cell reads **Version and status**, and in its Entry cell the first of the three status words **draft**, **reviewed** and **baselined**. When it reads baselined, the same cell says **accepted by** *the name* **on** *the date*, the date written `25 September 2026` or `2026-09-25`. The gate refuses a status other than baselined, a baselined document with no name or no date it can read, and a date that has not yet come (check 4) |
| The table headed `Goal` · `Its description` · `Its screen record` | in each row's Goal cell the goal's identifier between backticks, `UC-XX-01`. A form or a list of the model whose `use_case` names a goal — the part before any `/` — that this table does not hold is refused (check 5) |

Nothing else of the header is read by the gate. The document's own acceptance is what the owner
gives when he reads it; the gate reads only that it is recorded.

### The Lists table — check 6

| Column | What the gate reads, and what it compares it with |
|---|---|
| List | the identifier of the model's vocabulary between backticks, exactly one — or **not in the model** |
| Members in force | a whole number at the very start of the cell; when the cell begins otherwise ("not stated; 11 in the model"), the number of values the model's vocabulary holds. More than nine, and the model's vocabulary carries no `groups`: refused |
| Maintained or fixed | the first of **maintained** and **fixed**, compared with the vocabulary's `fixed` |
| Maintained by | for a list that is maintained, one role identifier between backticks, compared with the vocabulary's `maintained_by` or, where the model names none, the administrator role the kit gives the administration of lists of values to. Not read for a fixed list |
| Code shown as label | the first of **yes** and **no**, compared with the vocabulary's `display_code` |
| Categories | **none**, compared with a vocabulary that carries no `groups`; or the identifier of the vocabulary that holds its categories, between backticks, compared with `groups` — followed, if you give them, by the categories' codes, each between backticks, compared with that vocabulary's codes |
| Kept where · First values from · Depends on · Pattern | not read |

### The References table — check 7

| Column | What the gate reads, and what it compares it with |
|---|---|
| Place | the form and the field on it where the person picks the record, between backticks and written `form.field` — the field is the one the form places by its `attr` or its `id`. Or, for a place where the person picks a row of a list, the list's identifier alone; for such a place the gate reads nothing more than that the list is in the model. Or **not in the model** |
| Found by | whether the cell begins **a search**. A place found by a search that the model places as a drop-down, a group of radio buttons, a group of check-boxes or a lookup control is refused |
| Fills | the fields of the same form the choice fills, each between backticks, or **nothing**. Compared with what the model fills from the choice: the mappings of the form's `prefill` whose key source is this field (`source: currentField`), the mappings of the form's `lookup` that watches this field, and the fields this field's `config.populate` fills |
| Locks | the filled fields that are then locked, each between backticks, or **nothing**, or **everything it fills**. Compared with what the model locks: every field a search's `populate` fills, which the kit locks, and every field a prefill or a look-up fills that the form shows read-only |
| Record set · How many · Searched by · Result shows · When nothing is found · Pattern | not read |

### The Moves table — check 8

| Column | What the gate reads, and what it compares it with |
|---|---|
| Record | the entity whose lifecycle the move belongs to, between backticks |
| From · To | the identifiers of the two states between backticks, compared with the move's `from` and `to` |
| Made by (goal, act) | the goal and its act in words, and the move's identifier between backticks — the `id` of the transition in the model's lifecycle. Nothing else in this cell stands between backticks. Or **not in the model**, for a move the goals make that the model does not carry |
| Role | the identifiers of the roles that may make the move, between backticks, compared with the roles of the act that offers its button (the act's `roles`, or the move's own where the act names none) |
| Button | the button's words exactly as the model's act gives them as its `label`, with no quotation marks needed; where two acts make the same move, their words separated by `;`; or **none**, for a move no act of this increment makes, in which case the model may offer no button for it |
| Guard, in the words shown | not read |

The gate also reads the table the other way. Every button the model offers for a move — an entry
of a form's `acts` that names a `transition`, and, on a form that is not a create form and declares
no acts, the one button the kit gives it for each move a person makes that the form's roles may
make, labelled after the move — must stand in this table, on the row of that record and that move,
among its buttons. A button the table does not list is refused.

### The Read-only table

Not read by the gate. The owner and the writer of the model read it.

### The Acts table — check 9

| Column | What the gate reads, and what it compares it with |
|---|---|
| Stands on | the one form, list or navigation category of the model the act stands on, between backticks: a form whose `acts` carry an act that opens a form; a list whose row `actions` include an `open_form`; or a category whose menu opens the form. Or **not in the model** |
| Opens | the form the act opens, between backticks. The model must have such an act there |
| Carries | the entity whose record the act carries, between backticks, or **nothing**. A form's act carries its `carries`, or the form's own record; a list's row act carries the record of the row; a category's CRUD menu carries the record of the row it opens, and any other menu carries nothing |
| On a menu | the first of **yes** and **no**. For **no**, the form it opens may stand on no menu of a category that is not `hidden` |
| Act · Filled and locked · Returns to | not read |

